The traversal below visits every node once. It keeps two small pieces of information: whether the node is on the left edge and whether it is on the right edge.

A leaf is placed immediately. Left edge nodes are placed before their children, while right edge nodes are placed after their children. That naturally creates the correct order.

```python
class Solution:
    def boundary(self, root):
        if root is None:
            return []

        def leaf(node):
            return node.left is None and node.right is None

        answer = []

        def walk(node, left_edge, right_edge):
            if node is None:
                return

            if leaf(node):
                answer.append(node.data)
                return

            if left_edge:
                answer.append(node.data)

            walk(
                node.left,
                left_edge and node.left is not None,
                right_edge and node.right is None
            )
            walk(
                node.right,
                left_edge and node.left is None,
                right_edge and node.right is not None
            )

            if right_edge:
                answer.append(node.data)

        if leaf(root):
            return [root.data]

        answer.append(root.data)
        walk(root.left, True, False)
        walk(root.right, False, True)
        return answer
```

One edge case is a path-shaped tree. The missing-child checks pass the side flag to the only child, so every outside node is still included without adding a leaf twice.

Time complexity: O(n), with one visit per node.
Space complexity: O(h) because of recursive calls.

The tree is handled in three parts: the left side, all leaf nodes, and the right side. The right side is collected from top to bottom and then reversed, because boundary order is counter-clockwise.

The root is added first. A leaf root is returned only once.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    bool isLeaf(TreeNode* node) {
        return node != nullptr && node->left == nullptr && node->right == nullptr;
    }

    void addLeftBoundary(TreeNode* node, vector<int>& answer) {
        while (node != nullptr) {
            if (!isLeaf(node)) {
                answer.push_back(node->data);
            }
            node = (node->left != nullptr) ? node->left : node->right;
        }
    }

    void addLeaves(TreeNode* node, vector<int>& answer) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }
        if (isLeaf(node)) {
            answer.push_back(node->data);
            return;
        }
        addLeaves(node->left, answer);
        addLeaves(node->right, answer);
    }

    void addRightBoundary(TreeNode* node, vector<int>& answer) {
        vector<int> rightSide;
        while (node != nullptr) {
            if (!isLeaf(node)) {
                rightSide.push_back(node->data);
            }
            node = (node->right != nullptr) ? node->right : node->left;
        }
        reverse(rightSide.begin(), rightSide.end());
        answer.insert(answer.end(), rightSide.begin(), rightSide.end());
    }

public:
    vector<int> boundary(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int> answer;
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return answer;
        }

        if (!isLeaf(root)) {
            answer.push_back(root->data);
        }
        addLeftBoundary(root->left, answer);
        addLeaves(root, answer);
        addRightBoundary(root->right, answer);
        return answer;
    }
};
```

For a tree with root 1, left path 2, 4 and right path 3, 5, the result is built as 1, 2, 4, 5, 3.

Time complexity: O(n), since each part visits only a linear number of nodes.
Space complexity: O(h) for recursion and the temporary right boundary, not counting the returned list.

This version keeps the three pieces separate, but uses loops for both side paths. Leaf nodes are skipped while walking the sides, then collected with a small recursive function.

The right path goes into a stack. Popping it later gives the required bottom-up order.

```python
class Solution:
    def boundary(self, root):
        if root is None:
            return []

        def is_leaf(node):
            return node.left is None and node.right is None

        def add_leaves(node):
            if node is None:
                return
            if is_leaf(node):
                answer.append(node.data)
                return
            add_leaves(node.left)
            add_leaves(node.right)

        answer = []
        if not is_leaf(root):
            answer.append(root.data)

        node = root.left
        while node is not None:
            if not is_leaf(node):
                answer.append(node.data)
            node = node.left if node.left is not None else node.right

        add_leaves(root)

        right_nodes = []
        node = root.right
        while node is not None:
            if not is_leaf(node):
                right_nodes.append(node.data)
            node = node.right if node.right is not None else node.left

        while right_nodes:
            answer.append(right_nodes.pop())

        return answer
```

For input `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]`, the root and left side are added first, leaves are visited from left to right, and the right side is popped in reverse order.

A null tree returns an empty list, and a tree with one node returns that node once.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(h) for the right stack and leaf recursion.

Instead of marking nodes during one large traversal, this code first follows the two outside paths and then scans the tree for leaves. The left path is already in the right order. The right path is reversed before it is joined.

The root is treated separately so a single-node tree does not duplicate its value.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    private boolean isLeaf(TreeNode node) {
        return node != null && node.left == null && node.right == null;
    }

    private void collectLeaves(TreeNode node, List<Integer> answer) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }
        if (isLeaf(node)) {
            answer.add(node.data);
            return;
        }
        collectLeaves(node.left, answer);
        collectLeaves(node.right, answer);
    }

    public List<Integer> boundary(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }
        if (isLeaf(root)) {
            answer.add(root.data);
            return answer;
        }

        answer.add(root.data);

        TreeNode node = root.left;
        while (node != null) {
            if (!isLeaf(node)) {
                answer.add(node.data);
            }
            node = node.left != null ? node.left : node.right;
        }

        collectLeaves(root, answer);

        List<Integer> rightSide = new ArrayList<>();
        node = root.right;
        while (node != null) {
            if (!isLeaf(node)) {
                rightSide.add(node.data);
            }
            node = node.right != null ? node.right : node.left;
        }
        Collections.reverse(rightSide);
        answer.addAll(rightSide);

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The side walk always chooses the preferred child first, then uses the other child when the preferred one is missing. This handles uneven trees as well as complete ones.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(h) for the recursive leaf scan and the stored right path.

A single depth first walk can decide what to add by carrying two flags. The left flag means the node belongs to the outside left edge. The right flag does the same for the outside right edge.

Leaves are added during the middle of the walk. Right boundary nodes are added after their children, which puts them in bottom-up order.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    private boolean isLeaf(TreeNode node) {
        return node != null && node.left == null && node.right == null;
    }

    private void visit(TreeNode node, boolean isLeftEdge, boolean isRightEdge,
                       List<Integer> answer) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        boolean leaf = isLeaf(node);
        if (leaf) {
            answer.add(node.data);
            return;
        }

        if (isLeftEdge) {
            answer.add(node.data);
        }

        boolean leftKeepsLeft = isLeftEdge && node.left != null;
        boolean rightKeepsLeft = isLeftEdge && node.left == null;
        boolean leftKeepsRight = isRightEdge && node.right == null;
        boolean rightKeepsRight = isRightEdge && node.right != null;

        visit(node.left, leftKeepsLeft, leftKeepsRight, answer);
        visit(node.right, rightKeepsLeft, rightKeepsRight, answer);

        if (isRightEdge) {
            answer.add(node.data);
        }
    }

    public List<Integer> boundary(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }
        if (isLeaf(root)) {
            answer.add(root.data);
            return answer;
        }

        answer.add(root.data);
        visit(root.left, true, false, answer);
        visit(root.right, false, true, answer);
        return answer;
    }
}
```

The left child keeps the left flag whenever it exists. If it does not exist, the right child takes that flag. The same idea is used in reverse for the right side.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(h) for the recursion stack, where h is the tree height.

## Intuition
The boundary of a binary tree consists of three distinct parts — the left boundary (top-down), all leaf nodes (left-to-right), and the right boundary (bottom-up). Instead of trying to collect everything in one traversal, we can break the problem into three independent passes, each with a clear responsibility. The root is always included separately as the starting point of the boundary.

## Approach
We split the traversal into three parts. First, the root is added directly. Then addLeftBoundaryTraversal recursively walks down the left side (preferring left, falling back to right), adding all non-leaf nodes top-down. Next, addLeaves does a full DFS to collect all leaf nodes left-to-right. Finally, addRightBoundaryTraversal mirrors the left boundary logic on the right side, collecting non-leaf nodes into a temporary vector which is reversed before appending to get the required bottom-up order.

